{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00451","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00451","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00451","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dalal","familyName":"Alharthi","name":"Alharthi, Dalal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivan Roberto Kawaminami","familyName":"Garcia","name":"Garcia, Ivan Roberto Kawaminami","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Call to Action for a Secure-by-Design Generative AI Paradigm"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T03:05:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:23:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have gained widespread prominence, yet their vulnerability to prompt injection and other adversarial attacks remains a critical concern. This paper argues for a security-by-design AI paradigm that proactively mitigates LLM vulnerabilities while enhancing performance. To achieve this, we introduce PromptShield, an ontology-driven framework that ensures deterministic and secure prompt interactions. It standardizes user inputs through semantic validation, eliminating ambiguity and mitigating adversarial manipulation. To assess PromptShield's security and performance capabilities, we conducted an experiment on an agent-based system to analyze cloud logs within Amazon Web Services (AWS), containing 493 distinct events related to malicious activities and anomalies. By simulating prompt injection attacks and assessing the impact of deploying PromptShield, our results demonstrate a significant improvement in model security and performance, achieving precision, recall, and F1 scores of approximately 94%. Notably, the ontology-based framework not only mitigates adversarial threats but also enhances the overall performance and reliability of the system. Furthermore, PromptShield's modular and adaptable design ensures its applicability beyond cloud security, making it a robust solution for safeguarding generative AI applications across various domains. By laying the groundwork for AI safety standards and informing future policy development, this work stimulates a crucial dialogue on the pivotal role of deterministic prompt engineering and ontology-based validation in ensuring the safe and responsible deployment of LLMs in high-stakes environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00451","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:00:00Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:00:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:00:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00449","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00449","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00449","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koki","familyName":"Ryu","name":"Ryu, Koki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hitomi","familyName":"Yanaka","name":"Yanaka, Hitomi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Rating Prediction with Off-the-Shelf LLMs Using In-Context User Reviews"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T03:04:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:23:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Personalizing the outputs of large language models (LLMs) to align with individual user preferences is an active research area. However, previous studies have mainly focused on classification or ranking tasks and have not considered Likert-scale rating prediction, a regression task that requires both language and mathematical reasoning to be solved effectively. This task has significant industrial applications, but the utilization of LLMs remains underexplored, particularly regarding the capabilities of off-the-shelf LLMs. This study investigates the performance of off-the-shelf LLMs on rating prediction, providing different in-context information. Through comprehensive experiments with eight models across three datasets, we demonstrate that user-written reviews significantly improve the rating prediction performance of LLMs. This result is comparable to traditional methods like matrix factorization, highlighting the potential of LLMs as a promising solution for the cold-start problem. We also find that the reviews for concrete items are more effective than general preference descriptions that are not based on any specific item. Furthermore, we discover that prompting LLMs to first generate a hypothetical review enhances the rating prediction performance. Our code is available at https://github.com/ynklab/rating-prediction-with-reviews."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 PALS Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00449","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:59:57Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:59:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:59:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00446","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00446","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00446","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuling","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yuling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichun","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Yichun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beijun","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Beijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LongCodeZip: Compress Long Context for Code Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T02:54:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:22:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Code generation under long contexts is becoming increasingly critical as Large Language Models (LLMs) are required to reason over extensive information in the codebase. While recent advances enable code LLMs to process long inputs, high API costs and generation latency remain substantial bottlenecks. Existing context pruning techniques, such as LLMLingua, achieve promising results for general text but overlook code-specific structures and dependencies, leading to suboptimal performance in programming tasks. In this paper, we propose LongCodeZip, a novel plug-and-play code compression framework designed specifically for code LLMs. LongCodeZip employs a dual-stage strategy: (1) coarse-grained compression, which identifies and ranks function-level chunks using conditional perplexity with respect to the instruction, retaining only the most relevant functions; and (2) fine-grained compression, which segments retained functions into blocks based on perplexity and selects an optimal subset under an adaptive token budget to maximize relevance. Evaluations across multiple tasks, including code completion, summarization, and question answering, show that LongCodeZip consistently outperforms baseline methods, achieving up to a 5.6x compression ratio without degrading task performance. By effectively reducing context size while preserving essential information, LongCodeZip enables LLMs to better scale to real-world, large-scale code scenarios, advancing the efficiency and capability of code intelligence applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ASE 2025. Code available at https://github.com/YerbaPage/LongCodeZip"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00446","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:59:52Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:59:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:59:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00428","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00428","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00428","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seongjae","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Seongjae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong Bok","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Dong Bok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juho","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Juho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongseop","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dongseop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Won Hwa","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Won Hwa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunghoon","familyName":"Joo","name":"Joo, Sunghoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automated Structured Radiology Report Generation with Rich Clinical Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T02:14:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:21:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated structured radiology report generation (SRRG) from chest X-ray images offers significant potential to reduce workload of radiologists by generating reports in structured formats that ensure clarity, consistency, and adherence to clinical reporting standards. While radiologists effectively utilize available clinical contexts in their diagnostic reasoning, existing SRRG systems overlook these essential elements. This fundamental gap leads to critical problems including temporal hallucinations when referencing non-existent clinical contexts. To address these limitations, we propose contextualized SRRG (C-SRRG) that comprehensively incorporates rich clinical context for SRRG. We curate C-SRRG dataset by integrating comprehensive clinical context encompassing 1) multi-view X-ray images, 2) clinical indication, 3) imaging techniques, and 4) prior studies with corresponding comparisons based on patient histories. Through extensive benchmarking with state-of-the-art multimodal large language models, we demonstrate that incorporating clinical context with the proposed C-SRRG significantly improves report generation quality. We publicly release dataset, code, and checkpoints to facilitate future research for clinically-aligned automated RRG at https://github.com/vuno/contextualized-srrg."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"34 pages, 30 figures, preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00428","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:59:27Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:59:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:59:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00414","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00414","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00414","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhikun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vivek","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Vivek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thao","familyName":"Ha","name":"Ha, Thao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liesal","familyName":"Sharabi","name":"Sharabi, Liesal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RELATE-Sim: Leveraging Turning Point Theory and LLM Agents to Predict and Understand Long-Term Relationship Dynamics through Interactive Narrative Simulations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T01:50:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:19:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Most dating technologies optimize for getting together, not staying together. We present RELATE-Sim, a theory-grounded simulator that models how couples behave at consequential turning points-exclusivity talks, conflict-and-repair episodes, relocations-rather than static traits. Two persona-aligned LLM agents (one per partner) interact under a centralized Scene Master that frames each turning point as a compact set of realistic options, advances the narrative, and infers interpretable state changes and an auditable commitment estimate after each scene. On a longitudinal dataset of 71 couples with two-year follow-ups, simulation-aware predictions outperform a personas-only baseline while surfacing actionable markers (e.g., repair attempts acknowledged, clarity shifts) that explain why trajectories diverge. RELATE-Sim pushes the relationship research's focus from matchmaking to maintenance, providing a transparent, extensible platform for understanding and forecasting long-term relationship dynamics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 3 figures, Submitted to CHI 2026 Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00414","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:59:07Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:59:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:59:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00401","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00401","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00401","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shounak","familyName":"Sural","name":"Sural, Shounak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charles","familyName":"Kekeh","name":"Kekeh, Charles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenliang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wenliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federico","familyName":"Pecora","name":"Pecora, Federico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mouhacine","familyName":"Benosman","name":"Benosman, Mouhacine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Physics-Informed Neural Controlled Differential Equations for Scalable Long Horizon Multi-Agent Motion Forecasting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T01:27:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:18:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-horizon motion forecasting for multiple autonomous robots is challenging due to non-linear agent interactions, compounding prediction errors, and continuous-time evolution of dynamics. Learned dynamics of such a system can be useful in various applications such as travel time prediction, prediction-guided planning and generative simulation. In this work, we aim to develop an efficient trajectory forecasting model conditioned on multi-agent goals. Motivated by the recent success of physics-guided deep learning for partially known dynamical systems, we develop a model based on neural Controlled Differential Equations (CDEs) for long-horizon motion forecasting. Unlike discrete-time methods such as RNNs and transformers, neural CDEs operate in continuous time, allowing us to combine physics-informed constraints and biases to jointly model multi-robot dynamics. Our approach, named PINCoDE (Physics-Informed Neural Controlled Differential Equations), learns differential equation parameters that can be used to predict the trajectories of a multi-agent system starting from an initial condition. PINCoDE is conditioned on future goals and enforces physics constraints for robot motion over extended periods of time. We adopt a strategy that scales our model from 10 robots to 100 robots without the need for additional model parameters, while producing predictions with an average ADE below 0.5 m for a 1-minute horizon. Furthermore, progressive training with curriculum learning for our PINCoDE model results in a 2.7X reduction of forecasted pose error over 4 minute horizons compared to analytical models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00401","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:58:48Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:58:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:58:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00392","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00392","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00392","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Omer","familyName":"Latif","name":"Latif, Muhammad Omer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hayat","familyName":"Ullah","name":"Ullah, Hayat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Ali","familyName":"Shafique","name":"Shafique, Muhammad Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihua","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Zhihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Deep Learning Pipeline for Epilepsy Genomic Analysis Using GPT-2 XL and NVIDIA H100"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Genomics (q-bio.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T01:07:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:17:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Epilepsy is a chronic neurological condition characterized by recurrent seizures, with global prevalence estimated at 50 million people worldwide. While progress in high-throughput sequencing has allowed for broad-based transcriptomic profiling of brain tissues, the deciphering of these highly complex datasets remains one of the challenges. To address this issue, in this paper we propose a new analysis pipeline that integrates the power of deep learning strategies with GPU-acceleration computation for investigating Gene expression patterns in epilepsy. Specifically, our proposed approach employs GPT-2 XL, a transformer-based Large Language Model (LLM) with 1.5 billion parameters for genomic sequence analysis over the latest NVIDIA H100 Tensor Core GPUs based on Hopper architecture. Our proposed method enables efficient preprocessing of RNA sequence data, gene sequence encoding, and subsequent pattern identification. We conducted experiments on two epilepsy datasets including GEO accession GSE264537 and GSE275235. The obtained results reveal several significant transcriptomic modifications, including reduced hippocampal astrogliosis after ketogenic diet treatment as well as restored excitatory-inhibitory signaling equilibrium in zebrafish epilepsy model. Moreover, our results highlight the effectiveness of leveraging LLMs in combination with advanced hardware acceleration for transcriptomic characterization in neurological diseases."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00392","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:58:35Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:58:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:58:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00373","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00373","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00373","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlo","familyName":"Bosio","name":"Bosio, Carlo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo","familyName":"Guarrera","name":"Guarrera, Matteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto","familyName":"Sangiovanni-Vincentelli","name":"Sangiovanni-Vincentelli, Alberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark W.","familyName":"Mueller","name":"Mueller, Mark W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Combining Large Language Models and Gradient-Free Optimization for Automatic Control Policy Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T00:42:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:16:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language models (LLMs) have shown promise as generators of symbolic control policies, producing interpretable program-like representations through iterative search. However, these models are not capable of separating the functional structure of a policy from the numerical values it is parametrized by, thus making the search process slow and inefficient. We propose a hybrid approach that decouples structural synthesis from parameter optimization by introducing an additional optimization layer for local parameter search. In our method, the numerical parameters of LLM-generated programs are extracted and optimized numerically to maximize task performance. With this integration, an LLM iterates over the functional structure of programs, while a separate optimization loop is used to find a locally optimal set of parameters accompanying candidate programs. We evaluate our method on a set of control tasks, showing that it achieves higher returns and improved sample efficiency compared to purely LLM-guided search. We show that combining symbolic program synthesis with numerical optimization yields interpretable yet high-performing policies, bridging the gap between language-model-guided design and classical control tuning. Our code is available at https://sites.google.com/berkeley.edu/colmo."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00373","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:58:07Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:58:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:58:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00368","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00368","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00368","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andy","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Andy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Watson","name":"Watson, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anton","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Anton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satwik","familyName":"Bhattamishra","name":"Bhattamishra, Satwik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose","familyName":"Llarena","name":"Llarena, Jose","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Merrill","name":"Merrill, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emile Dos Santos","familyName":"Ferreira","name":"Ferreira, Emile Dos Santos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anej","familyName":"Svete","name":"Svete, Anej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Chiang","name":"Chiang, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Transformer Cookbook"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T00:25:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:15:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present the transformer cookbook: a collection of techniques for directly encoding algorithms into a transformer's parameters. This work addresses the steep learning curve of such endeavors, a problem exacerbated by a fragmented literature where key results are scattered across numerous papers. In particular, we synthesize this disparate body of findings into a curated set of recipes that demonstrate how to implement everything from basic arithmetic in feed-forward layers to complex data routing via self-attention. Our mise en place of formulations is for both newcomers seeking an accessible entry point and experts in need of a systematic reference. This unified presentation of transformer constructions provides a foundation for future work spanning theoretical research in computational complexity to empirical investigations in architecture design and interpretability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"39 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00368","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:57:59Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:58:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:58:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00347","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00347","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00347","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huitao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Huitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanting","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guanting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"In-Context Curiosity: Distilling Exploration for Decision-Pretrained Transformers on Bandit Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T23:17:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:14:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) continue to grow in capability, there is increasing interest in incorporating them into decision-making tasks. A common pipeline for this is Decision-Pretrained Transformers (DPTs). However, existing training methods for DPTs often struggle to generalize beyond their pretraining data distribution. To explore mitigation of this limitation, we propose in-context curiosity -- a lightweight, exploration-inspired regularizer for offline pretraining -- and introduce the Prediction-Powered Transformer (PPT) framework. PPT augments DPT with an auxiliary reward predictor, using prediction error as an intrinsic curiosity signal to encourage broader exploration during training. In proof-of-concept experiments on Gaussian multi-armed bandits, PPT shows improved robustness: it moderates the performance degradation observed in DPT when test environments exhibit higher variance in reward, particularly when pretraining data has limited diversity. While the quality of offline data remain fundamental, our preliminary results suggest that curiosity-driven pretraining offers a promising direction for enhancing out-of-distribution generalization in in-context RL agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00347","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:57:30Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:57:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:57:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00345","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00345","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00345","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiping","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Yiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Martens","name":"Martens, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianqiao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Jianqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ziqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peyman","familyName":"Moghadam","name":"Moghadam, Peyman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hemanth","familyName":"Saratchandran","name":"Saratchandran, Hemanth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Lucey","name":"Lucey, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cutting the Skip: Training Residual-Free Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T23:07:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:14:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers have achieved remarkable success across a wide range of applications, a feat often attributed to their scalability. Yet training them without skip (residual) connections remains notoriously difficult. While skips stabilize optimization, they also disrupt the hierarchical structure of representations, raising the long-standing question of whether transformers can be trained efficiently without them. In this work, we address this problem by analyzing the Jacobian of a skipless transformer block, showing why skips improve conditioning and revealing that their stabilization benefits can be recovered through a principled initialization strategy. Building on this insight, we introduce the first method that enables stable and efficient training of skipless transformers without altering the standard architecture. We validate our approach on Vision Transformers (ViTs) in both supervised and self-supervised settings, demonstrating that skipless ViTs trained with our initialization overcome the usual optimization barriers, learn richer hierarchical representations, and outperform strong baselines, that incorporate skip connections, on dense prediction benchmarks. These results show that skip connections are not a fundamental requirement for training ViTs and open new avenues for hierarchical representation learning in vision models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00345","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:57:27Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:57:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:57:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00339","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00339","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00339","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T. James","familyName":"Brandt","name":"Brandt, T. James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Navigating the Synchrony-Stability Frontier in Adaptive Chatbots"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; H.5.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T22:50:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:14:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Adaptive chatbots that mimic a user's linguistic style can build rapport and engagement, yet unconstrained mimicry risks an agent that feels unstable or sycophantic. We present a computational evaluation framework that makes the core design tension explicit: balancing moment-to-moment linguistic synchrony against long-term persona stability. Using an 8-dimensional style vector and a closed-loop \"base+delta\" prompting architecture, we simulate and compare explicit adaptation policies - Uncapped, Cap, Exponential Moving Average (EMA), Dead-Band, and Hybrids - on a human-log dataset. Our analysis maps a clear Pareto frontier: bounded policies achieve substantial gains in stability at a modest cost to synchrony. For example, a Hybrid (EMA+Cap) raises stability from 0.542 to 0.878 (+62%) while reducing synchrony by only 17%. We confirm this trade-off through large-scale replications on three public corpora (DailyDialog, Persona-Chat, EmpatheticDialogues) and LLM-in-the-loop validation across two model families. Furthermore, we quantify \"prompt legibility,\" showing that frontier policies reduce instruction churn and cut jarring register flips (major tone changes) from 0.254 to 0.092, yielding systems that are easier to reason about and maintain. Taken together, our framework provides a general evaluation harness for style adaptation; a systematic ablation that identifies Pareto-efficient policies; robust validation across diverse datasets and models; and novel legibility metrics linking policy choices to system maintainability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"pages; 9 tables; 7 figures; code \u0026amp; analysis artifact: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17238269; under review at ACM IUI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00339","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:57:18Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:57:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:57:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00326","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00326","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00326","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassen","familyName":"Dhrif","name":"Dhrif, Hassen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning-Aware Prompt Orchestration: A Foundation Model for Multi-Agent Language Model Coordination"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T22:33:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:13:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The emergence of large language models has enabled sophisticated multi-agent systems, yet coordinating their reasoning capabilities through prompt engineering remains challenging. We present a theoretically-grounded framework for dynamic prompt orchestration that enhances reasoning across multiple specialized agents. This framework addresses three core challenges: logical consistency preservation during agent transitions, reasoning-aware prompt adaptation, and scalable coordination of distributed inference.\n Our approach formalizes agent states using prompt templates, reasoning context vectors, and capability matrices. We prove system convergence to stable coordination patterns when step sizes satisfy $α\u0026lt; \\frac{1}{2L}$ where $L$ is the Lipschitz constant of the state transition function. We implement this through a distributed architecture that dynamically routes reasoning tasks while maintaining semantic coherence.\n Experimental results on 1,000 synthetic multi-agent conversations demonstrate a 42% reduction in reasoning latency, a 23% improvement in logical consistency measured by ROUGE-L score, and an 89% success rate for task completion without context loss across agent transitions. Ablation studies identify the consensus mechanism as the primary performance driver, while revealing limitations: performance degrades beyond 10 agent transitions, and the system requires 76.5GB memory for 1,000 concurrent agents. These findings establish a new paradigm for scalable reasoning in multi-agent systems, providing theoretical foundations for understanding reasoning emergence across coordinated language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00326","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:57:00Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:57:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:57:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00324","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00324","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00324","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucas","familyName":"Roberts","name":"Roberts, Lucas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Denisa","familyName":"Roberts","name":"Roberts, Denisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Which Programming Language and Model Work Best With LLM-as-a-Judge For Code Retrieval?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T22:31:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:13:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3767695.3769503","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Code search is an important information retrieval application. Benefits of better code search include faster new developer on-boarding, reduced software maintenance, and ease of understanding for large repositories. Despite improvements in search algorithms and search benchmarks, the domain of code search has lagged behind. One reason is the high cost of human annotation for code queries and answers. While humans may annotate search results in general text QA systems, code annotations require specialized knowledge of a programming language (PL), as well as domain specific software engineering knowledge. In this work we study the use of Large Language Models (LLMs) to retrieve code at the level of functions and to generate annotations for code search results. We compare the impact of the retriever representation (sparse vs. semantic), programming language, and LLM by comparing human annotations across several popular languages (C, Java, Javascript, Go, and Python). We focus on repositories that implement common data structures likely to be implemented in any PLs. For the same human annotations, we compare several LLM-as-a-Judge models to evaluate programming language and other affinities between LLMs. We find that the chosen retriever and PL exhibit affinities that can be leveraged to improve alignment of human and AI relevance determinations, with significant performance implications. We also find differences in representation (sparse vs. semantic) across PLs that impact alignment of human and AI relevance determinations. We propose using transpilers to bootstrap scalable code search benchmark datasets in other PLs and in a case study demonstrate that human-AI relevance agreement rates largely match the (worst case) human-human agreement under study. The application code used in this work is available at \\href{https://github.com/rlucas7/code-searcher/}{this github repo}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as a full paper at SIGIR-AP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00324","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-02T01:56:57Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:56:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:56:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00313","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00313","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00313","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanmay","familyName":"Khandelwal","name":"Khandelwal, Tanmay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Magdalena","familyName":"Fuentes","name":"Fuentes, Magdalena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Post-Training Quantization for Audio Diffusion Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T22:09:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:13:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion Transformers (DiTs) enable high-quality audio synthesis but are often computationally intensive and require substantial storage, which limits their practical deployment. In this paper, we present a comprehensive evaluation of post-training quantization (PTQ) techniques for audio DiTs, analyzing the trade-offs between static and dynamic quantization schemes. We explore two practical extensions (1) a denoising-timestep-aware smoothing method that adapts quantization scales per-input-channel and timestep to mitigate activation outliers, and (2) a lightweight low-rank adapter (LoRA)-based branch derived from singular value decomposition (SVD) to compensate for residual weight errors. Using Stable Audio Open we benchmark W8A8 and W4A8 configurations across objective metrics and human perceptual ratings. Our results show that dynamic quantization preserves fidelity even at lower precision, while static methods remain competitive with lower latency. Overall, our findings show that low-precision DiTs can retain high-fidelity generation while reducing memory usage by up to 79%."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 4 figures, accepted at IEEE Workshop on Applications of Signal Processing to Audio and Acoustics (WASPAA) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00313","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:56:41Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:56:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:56:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00311","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00311","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00311","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan Shen","familyName":"Tay","name":"Tay, Yuan Shen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Howard","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Howard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinhao","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Jinhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziwei","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Ziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Jordan","name":"Jordan, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CORTEX: Collaborative LLM Agents for High-Stakes Alert Triage"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T22:09:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:13:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Security Operations Centers (SOCs) are overwhelmed by tens of thousands of daily alerts, with only a small fraction corresponding to genuine attacks. This overload creates alert fatigue, leading to overlooked threats and analyst burnout. Classical detection pipelines are brittle and context-poor, while recent LLM-based approaches typically rely on a single model to interpret logs, retrieve context, and adjudicate alerts end-to-end -- an approach that struggles with noisy enterprise data and offers limited transparency. We propose CORTEX, a multi-agent LLM architecture for high-stakes alert triage in which specialized agents collaborate over real evidence: a behavior-analysis agent inspects activity sequences, evidence-gathering agents query external systems, and a reasoning agent synthesizes findings into an auditable decision. To support training and evaluation, we release a dataset of fine-grained SOC investigations from production environments, capturing step-by-step analyst actions and linked tool outputs. Across diverse enterprise scenarios, CORTEX substantially reduces false positives and improves investigation quality over state-of-the-art single-agent LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00311","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:56:38Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:56:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:56:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00300","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00300","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00300","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Serena Gomez","familyName":"Wannaz","name":"Wannaz, Serena Gomez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ICL Optimized Fragility"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T21:43:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:12:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"ICL guides are known to improve task-specific performance, but their impact on cross-domain cognitive abilities remains unexplored. This study examines how ICL guides affect reasoning across different knowledge domains using six variants of the GPT-OSS:20b model: one baseline model and five ICL configurations (simple, chain-of-thought, random, appended text, and symbolic language). The models were subjected to 840 tests spanning general knowledge questions, logic riddles, and a mathematical olympiad problem. Statistical analysis (ANOVA) revealed significant behavioral modifications (p less than 0.001) across ICL variants, demonstrating a phenomenon termed \"optimized fragility.\" ICL models achieved 91%-99% accuracy on general knowledge tasks while showing degraded performance on complex reasoning problems, with accuracy dropping to 10-43% on riddles compared to 43% for the baseline model. Notably, no significant differences emerged on the olympiad problem (p=0.2173), suggesting that complex mathematical reasoning remains unaffected by ICL optimization. These findings indicate that ICL guides create systematic trade-offs between efficiency and reasoning flexibility, with important implications for LLM deployment and AI safety."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00300","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:56:23Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:56:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:56:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00296","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00296","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00296","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guy","familyName":"Bar-Shalom","name":"Bar-Shalom, Guy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabrizio","familyName":"Frasca","name":"Frasca, Fabrizio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaniv","familyName":"Galron","name":"Galron, Yaniv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yftah","familyName":"Ziser","name":"Ziser, Yftah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haggai","familyName":"Maron","name":"Maron, Haggai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Token Probes: Hallucination Detection via Activation Tensors with ACT-ViT"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T21:37:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:12:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Detecting hallucinations in Large Language Model-generated text is crucial for their safe deployment. While probing classifiers show promise, they operate on isolated layer-token pairs and are LLM-specific, limiting their effectiveness and hindering cross-LLM applications. In this paper, we introduce a novel approach to address these shortcomings. We build on the natural sequential structure of activation data in both axes (layers $\\times$ tokens) and advocate treating full activation tensors akin to images. We design ACT-ViT, a Vision Transformer-inspired model that can be effectively and efficiently applied to activation tensors and supports training on data from multiple LLMs simultaneously. Through comprehensive experiments encompassing diverse LLMs and datasets, we demonstrate that ACT-ViT consistently outperforms traditional probing techniques while remaining extremely efficient for deployment. In particular, we show that our architecture benefits substantially from multi-LLM training, achieves strong zero-shot performance on unseen datasets, and can be transferred effectively to new LLMs through fine-tuning. Full code is available at https://github.com/BarSGuy/ACT-ViT."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00296","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:56:17Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:56:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:56:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00288","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00288","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00288","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ľuboš","familyName":"Kriš","name":"Kriš, Ľuboš","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaroslav","familyName":"Kopčan","name":"Kopčan, Jaroslav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiwei","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Qiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrej","familyName":"Ridzik","name":"Ridzik, Andrej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcel","familyName":"Veselý","name":"Veselý, Marcel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Tamajka","name":"Tamajka, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"o-MEGA: Optimized Methods for Explanation Generation and Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T21:08:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:11:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The proliferation of transformer-based language models has revolutionized NLP domain while simultaneously introduced significant challenges regarding model transparency and trustworthiness. The complexity of achieving explainable systems in this domain is evidenced by the extensive array of explanation methods and evaluation metrics developed by researchers. To address the challenge of selecting optimal explainability approaches, we present \\textbf{\\texttt{o-mega}}, a hyperparameter optimization tool designed to automatically identify the most effective explainable AI methods and their configurations within the semantic matching domain. We evaluate o-mega on a post-claim matching pipeline using a curated dataset of social media posts paired with refuting claims. Our tool systematically explores different explainable methods and their hyperparameters, demonstrating improved transparency in automated fact-checking systems. As a result, such automated optimization of explanation methods can significantly enhance the interpretability of claim-matching models in critical applications such as misinformation detection, contributing to more trustworthy and transparent AI systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00288","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:56:06Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:56:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:56:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00283","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00283","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00283","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huanshu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Huanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sawyer D.","familyName":"Campbell","name":"Campbell, Sawyer D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob T.","familyName":"Young","name":"Young, Jacob T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Douglas H.","familyName":"Werner","name":"Werner, Douglas H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data driven approaches in nanophotonics: A review of AI-enabled metadevices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T21:03:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:10:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1039/d5nr02043c","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Data-driven approaches have revolutionized the design and optimization of photonic metadevices by harnessing advanced artificial intelligence methodologies. This review takes a model-centric perspective that synthesizes emerging design strategies and delineates how traditional trial-and-error and computationally intensive electromagnetic simulations are being supplanted by deep learning frameworks that efficiently navigate expansive design spaces. We discuss artificial intelligence implementation in several metamaterial design aspects from high-degree-of-freedom design to large language model-assisted design. By addressing challenges such as transformer model implementation, fabrication limitations, and intricate mutual coupling effects, these AI-enabled strategies not only streamline the forward modeling process but also offer robust pathways for the realization of multifunctional and fabrication-friendly nanophotonic devices. This review further highlights emerging opportunities and persistent challenges, setting the stage for next-generation strategies in nanophotonic engineering."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00283","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-02T01:55:58Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:55:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:55:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00276","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00276","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00276","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joe","familyName":"Barrow","name":"Barrow, Joe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raj","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Raj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Misha","familyName":"Kharkovski","name":"Kharkovski, Misha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Davies","name":"Davies, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan","familyName":"Schmitt","name":"Schmitt, Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SafePassage: High-Fidelity Information Extraction with Black Box LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T20:56:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:10:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Black box large language models (LLMs) make information extraction (IE) easy to configure, but hard to trust. Unlike traditional information extraction pipelines, the information \"extracted\" is not guaranteed to be grounded in the document. To prevent this, this paper introduces the notion of a \"safe passage\": context generated by the LLM that is both grounded in the document and consistent with the extracted information. This is operationalized via a three-step pipeline, SafePassage, which consists of: (1) an LLM extractor that generates structured entities and their contexts from a document, (2) a string-based global aligner, and (3) a scoring model. Results show that using these three parts in conjunction reduces hallucinations by up to 85% on information extraction tasks with minimal risk of flagging non-hallucinations. High agreement between the SafePassage pipeline and human judgments of extraction quality mean that the pipeline can be dually used to evaluate LLMs. Surprisingly, results also show that using a transformer encoder fine-tuned on a small number of task-specific examples can outperform an LLM scoring model at flagging unsafe passages. These annotations can be collected in as little as 1-2 hours."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00276","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:55:48Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:55:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:55:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00268","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00268","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00268","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhexiong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhexiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diane","familyName":"Litman","name":"Litman, Diane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Layer-wise LLM Fine-tuning for Revision Intention Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T20:42:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:09:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown extraordinary success across various text generation tasks; however, their potential for simple yet essential text classification remains underexplored, as LLM pre-training tends to emphasize generation over classification. While LLMs with instruction tuning can transform classification into a generation task, they often struggle to categorize nuanced texts. One such example is text revision, which involves nuanced edits between pairs of texts. Although simply fine-tuning LLMs for revision classification seems plausible, it requires a large amount of revision annotations, which are exceptionally expensive and scarce in the community. To address this issue, we introduce a plug-and-play layer-wise parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) framework, i.e., IR-Tuning, which fine-tunes a subset of important LLM layers that are dynamically selected based on their gradient norm distribution, while freezing those of redundant layers. Extensive experiments suggest that IR-Tuning surpasses several layer-wise PEFT baselines over diverse text revisions, while achieving fast convergence, low GPU memory consumption, and effectiveness on small revision corpora."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In The Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP), November 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00268","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:55:36Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:55:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:55:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00263","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00263","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00263","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuohang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhuohang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaowei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengyu","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Chengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guowang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Guowang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katayoon","familyName":"Goshvadi","name":"Goshvadi, Katayoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dale","familyName":"Schuurmans","name":"Schuurmans, Dale","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Palangi","name":"Palangi, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwen","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Palash","familyName":"Goyal","name":"Goyal, Palash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murat","familyName":"Kantarcioglu","name":"Kantarcioglu, Murat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bradley A.","familyName":"Malin","name":"Malin, Bradley A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Judging with Confidence: Calibrating Autoraters to Preference Distributions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T20:36:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:09:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The alignment of large language models (LLMs) with human values increasingly relies on using other LLMs as automated judges, or ``autoraters''. However, their reliability is limited by a foundational issue: they are trained on discrete preference labels, forcing a single ground truth onto tasks that are often subjective, ambiguous, or nuanced. We argue that a reliable autorater must learn to model the full distribution of preferences defined by a target population. In this paper, we propose a general framework for calibrating probabilistic autoraters to any given preference distribution. We formalize the problem and present two learning methods tailored to different data conditions: 1) a direct supervised fine-tuning for dense, probabilistic labels, and 2) a reinforcement learning approach for sparse, binary labels. Our empirical results show that finetuning autoraters with a distribution-matching objective leads to verbalized probability predictions that are better aligned with the target preference distribution, with improved calibration and significantly lower positional bias, all while preserving performance on objective tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00263","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:55:29Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:55:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:55:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00261","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00261","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00261","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaojing","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Chaojing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael A.","familyName":"Rosenberg","name":"Rosenberg, Michael A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emerson","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Emerson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ding","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Ding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Electrocardiogram-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T20:32:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:09:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Interest in generative Electrocardiogram-Language Models (ELMs) is growing, as they can produce textual responses conditioned on ECG signals and textual queries. Unlike traditional classifiers that output label probabilities, ELMs are more versatile, supporting domain-specific tasks (e.g., waveform analysis, diagnosis, prognosis) as well as general tasks (e.g., open-ended questions, dialogue). Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), widely used in Large Language Models (LLMs) to ground LLM outputs in retrieved knowledge, helps reduce hallucinations and improve natural language generation (NLG). However, despite its promise, no open-source implementation or systematic study of RAG pipeline design for ELMs currently exists. To address this gap, we present the first open-source RAG pipeline for ELMs, along with baselines and ablation studies for NLG. Experiments on three public datasets show that ELMs with RAG consistently improves performance over non-RAG baselines and highlights key ELM design considerations. Our code is available at: https://github.com/willxxy/ECG-Bench."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 2 figures; Submitted to ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00261","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:55:26Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:55:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:55:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00258","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00258","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00258","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Buu","familyName":"Phan","name":"Phan, Buu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reza","familyName":"Ebrahimi","name":"Ebrahimi, Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjay","familyName":"Haresh","name":"Haresh, Sanjay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roland","familyName":"Memisevic","name":"Memisevic, Roland","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Delayed Attention Training Improves Length Generalization in Transformer--RNN Hybrids"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T20:31:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:09:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study length generalization in sequence models on a composite problem involving both state tracking and associative recall. Prior work finds that recurrent networks handle state tracking well but struggle with recall, whereas Transformers excel at recall yet fail to extend state-tracking capabilities to longer sequences. Motivated by the complementary strengths of these architectures, we construct hybrid models integrating recurrent and attention-based components, and train them on the combined task to evaluate whether both capabilities can be preserved. Our results reveal that, in such hybrids, the Transformer component tends to exploit shortcut solutions, leading to poor length generalization. We identify this shortcut reliance as a key obstacle and propose a simple yet effective training strategy -- delaying the training of the attention layers -- that mitigates this effect and significantly improves length generalization performance. Our experiments show that this approach enables hybrid models to achieve near-perfect accuracy ($\u0026gt;90\\%$) on hybrid sequences three times longer than those used during training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00258","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:55:21Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:55:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:55:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00255","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00255","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00255","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Monishwaran","familyName":"Maheswaran","name":"Maheswaran, Monishwaran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Carini","name":"Carini, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Federmann","name":"Federmann, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony","familyName":"Diaz","name":"Diaz, Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TASER: Translation Assessment via Systematic Evaluation and Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T20:27:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:09:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce TASER (Translation Assessment via Systematic Evaluation and Reasoning), a metric that uses Large Reasoning Models (LRMs) for automated translation quality assessment. TASER harnesses the explicit reasoning capabilities of LRMs to conduct systematic, step-by-step evaluation of translation quality. We evaluate TASER on the WMT24 Metrics Shared Task across both reference-based and reference-free scenarios, demonstrating state-of-the-art performance. In system-level evaluation, TASER achieves the highest soft pairwise accuracy in both reference-based and reference-free settings, outperforming all existing metrics. At the segment level, TASER maintains competitive performance with our reference-free variant ranking as the top-performing metric among all reference-free approaches. Our experiments reveal that structured prompting templates yield superior results with LRMs compared to the open-ended approaches that proved optimal for traditional LLMs. We evaluate o3, a large reasoning model from OpenAI, with varying reasoning efforts, providing insights into the relationship between reasoning depth and evaluation quality. The explicit reasoning process in LRMs offers interpretability and visibility, addressing a key limitation of existing automated metrics. Our results demonstrate that Large Reasoning Models show a measurable advancement in translation quality assessment, combining improved accuracy with transparent evaluation across diverse language pairs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00255","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:55:17Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:55:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:55:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00206","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00206","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00206","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanda","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zhanda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qidong","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Qidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaoyao","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yaoyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gennady","familyName":"Pekhimenko","name":"Pekhimenko, Gennady","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LoRAFusion: Efficient LoRA Fine-Tuning for LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:26:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:07:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3767295.3769331","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) has become the leading Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) method for Large Language Models (LLMs), as it significantly reduces GPU memory usage while maintaining competitive fine-tuned model quality on downstream tasks. Despite these benefits, we identify two key inefficiencies in existing LoRA fine-tuning systems. First, they incur substantial runtime overhead due to redundant memory accesses on large activation tensors. Second, they miss the opportunity to concurrently fine-tune multiple independent LoRA adapters that share the same base model on the same set of GPUs. This leads to missed performance gains such as reduced pipeline bubbles, better communication overlap, and improved GPU load balance.\n To address these issues, we introduce LoRAFusion, an efficient LoRA fine-tuning system for LLMs. At the kernel level, we propose a graph-splitting method that fuses memory-bound operations. This design eliminates unnecessary memory accesses and preserves the performance of compute-bound GEMMs without incurring the cost of recomputation or synchronization. At the scheduling level, LoRAFusion introduces an adaptive batching algorithm for multi-job fine-tuning. It first splits LoRA adapters into groups to intentionally stagger batch execution across jobs, and then solves a bin-packing problem within each group to generate balanced, dependency-aware microbatches. LoRAFusion achieves up to $1.96\\times$ ($1.47\\times$ on average) end-to-end speedup compared to Megatron-LM, and up to $1.46\\times$ ($1.29\\times$ on average) improvement over mLoRA, the state-of-the-art multi-LoRA fine-tuning system. Our fused kernel achieves up to $1.39\\times$ ($1.27\\times$ on average) kernel performance improvement and can directly serve as a plug-and-play replacement in existing LoRA systems. We open-source LoRAFusion at https://github.com/CentML/lorafusion."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EuroSys 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00206","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-02T01:54:03Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:54:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:54:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00205","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00205","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00205","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongtian","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Zhongtian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenghao","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Chenghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anoushka","familyName":"Harit","name":"Harit, Anoushka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongmin","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jongmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantifying Semantic Shift in Financial NLP: Robust Metrics for Market Prediction Stability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Finance (q-fin.CP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:25:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:07:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Financial news is essential for accurate market prediction, but evolving narratives across macroeconomic regimes introduce semantic and causal drift that weaken model reliability. We present an evaluation framework to quantify robustness in financial NLP under regime shifts. The framework defines four metrics: (1) Financial Causal Attribution Score (FCAS) for alignment with causal cues, (2) Patent Cliff Sensitivity (PCS) for sensitivity to semantic perturbations, (3) Temporal Semantic Volatility (TSV) for drift in latent text representations, and (4) NLI-based Logical Consistency Score (NLICS) for entailment coherence. Applied to LSTM and Transformer models across four economic periods (pre-COVID, COVID, post-COVID, and rate hike), the metrics reveal performance degradation during crises. Semantic volatility and Jensen-Shannon divergence correlate with prediction error. Transformers are more affected by drift, while feature-enhanced variants improve generalisation. A GPT-4 case study confirms that alignment-aware models better preserve causal and logical consistency. The framework supports auditability, stress testing, and adaptive retraining in financial AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The 6th ACM International Conference on Al in Finance"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00205","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:54:02Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:54:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:54:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00194","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00194","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00194","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prasanna","familyName":"Parthasarathi","name":"Parthasarathi, Prasanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathieu","familyName":"Reymond","name":"Reymond, Mathieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boxing","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Boxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarath","familyName":"Chandar","name":"Chandar, Sarath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GRPO-$λ$: Credit Assignment improves LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:11:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:06:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed for tasks requiring complex reasoning, prompting significant interest in improving their reasoning abilities through post-training. Especially RL based methods using verifiable reward, like the state-of-the-art GRPO, have shown to tremendously improve reasoning behaviors when applied as post-training methods. However, the lack of an explicit reward or critic model limits GRPO's ability to assign fine-grained credit across token sequences. In this work, we present GRPO-$λ$, a novel extension to GRPO that enhances credit assignment in RL finetuning of LLMs for complex reasoning tasks. We approximate learning from $λ$-return with a reformulation of eligibility traces using token-level log-probabilities applied after each sequence generation, and a novel critic-free approximation of the temporal-difference error. We introduce a few variations for the weighting of the $λ$-return, and their applications to the eligibility-trace, where all the variations provide significant gains over GRPO. We compare GRPO-$λ$ against GRPO by training models from 1.5B to 7B parameters on $4$ different math reasoning datasets. The training plots demonstrate 30-40% improved performance during RL training on both LLaMA-3.1 and Qwen-2.5 architectures. Finally, we show that with GRPO-$λ$, the resulting average performance on AIME24, Math500, OlympiadMath, MinervaMath, and AMC improves over GRPO by over $3$ points and a $4.5$ points improvement on the 7B model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00194","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:53:46Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:53:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:53:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00184","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00184","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00184","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyan","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Xiaoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Itamar","familyName":"Pres","name":"Pres, Itamar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntian","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Yuntian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhao","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Chenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stuart","familyName":"Shieber","name":"Shieber, Stuart","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fernanda","familyName":"Viégas","name":"Viégas, Fernanda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Wattenberg","name":"Wattenberg, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Why Can't Transformers Learn Multiplication? Reverse-Engineering Reveals Long-Range Dependency Pitfalls"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:03:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:06:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language models are increasingly capable, yet still fail at a seemingly simple task of multi-digit multiplication. In this work, we study why, by reverse-engineering a model that successfully learns multiplication via \\emph{implicit chain-of-thought}, and report three findings: (1) Evidence of long-range structure: Logit attributions and linear probes indicate that the model encodes the necessary long-range dependencies for multi-digit multiplication. (2) Mechanism: the model encodes long-range dependencies using attention to construct a directed acyclic graph to ``cache'' and ``retrieve'' pairwise partial products. (3) Geometry: the model implements partial products in attention heads by forming Minkowski sums between pairs of digits, and digits are represented using a Fourier basis, both of which are intuitive and efficient representations that the standard fine-tuning model lacks. With these insights, we revisit the learning dynamics of standard fine-tuning and find that the model converges to a local optimum that lacks the required long-range dependencies. We further validate this understanding by introducing an auxiliary loss that predicts the ``running sum'' via a linear regression probe, which provides an inductive bias that enables the model to successfully learn multi-digit multiplication. In summary, by reverse-engineering the mechanisms of an implicit chain-of-thought model we uncover a pitfall for learning long-range dependencies in Transformers and provide an example of how the correct inductive bias can address this issue."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00184","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:53:31Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:53:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:53:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00174","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00174","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00174","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rik","familyName":"Koncel-Kedziorski","name":"Koncel-Kedziorski, Rik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brihi","familyName":"Joshi","name":"Joshi, Brihi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim","familyName":"Paek","name":"Paek, Tim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PrimeX: A Dataset of Worldview, Opinion, and Explanation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T18:50:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:05:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As the adoption of language models advances, so does the need to better represent individual users to the model. Are there aspects of an individual's belief system that a language model can utilize for improved alignment? Following prior research, we investigate this question in the domain of opinion prediction by developing PrimeX, a dataset of public opinion survey data from 858 US residents with two additional sources of belief information: written explanations from the respondents for why they hold specific opinions, and the Primal World Belief survey for assessing respondent worldview. We provide an extensive initial analysis of our data and show the value of belief explanations and worldview for personalizing language models. Our results demonstrate how the additional belief information in PrimeX can benefit both the NLP and psychological research communities, opening up avenues for further study."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Main"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00174","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:53:17Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:53:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:53:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00161","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00161","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00161","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kimihiro","familyName":"Hasegawa","name":"Hasegawa, Kimihiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wiradee","familyName":"Imrattanatrai","name":"Imrattanatrai, Wiradee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masaki","familyName":"Asada","name":"Asada, Masaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ken","familyName":"Fukuda","name":"Fukuda, Ken","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teruko","familyName":"Mitamura","name":"Mitamura, Teruko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TAMA: Tool-Augmented Multimodal Agent for Procedural Activity Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T18:34:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:04:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Procedural activity assistants potentially support humans in a variety of settings, from our daily lives, e.g., cooking or assembling flat-pack furniture, to professional situations, e.g., manufacturing or biological experiments. Despite its potential use cases, the system development tailored for such an assistant is still underexplored. In this paper, we propose a novel framework, called TAMA, a Tool-Augmented Multimodal Agent, for procedural activity understanding. TAMA enables interleaved multimodal reasoning by making use of multimedia-returning tools in a training-free setting. Our experimental result on the multimodal procedural QA dataset, ProMQA-Assembly, shows that our approach can improve the performance of vision-language models, especially GPT-5 and MiMo-VL. Furthermore, our ablation studies provide empirical support for the effectiveness of two features that characterize our framework, multimedia-returning tools and agentic flexible tool selection. We believe our proposed framework and experimental results facilitate the thinking with images paradigm for video and multimodal tasks, let alone the development of procedural activity assistants."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages. Code: https://github.com/kimihiroh/tama"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00161","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:52:57Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:52:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:52:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00143","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00143","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00143","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eugene","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Eugene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawn","familyName":"Lawrie","name":"Lawrie, Dawn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Orion","familyName":"Weller","name":"Weller, Orion","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Mayfield","name":"Mayfield, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HLTCOE at TREC 2024 NeuCLIR Track"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T18:16:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:03:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The HLTCOE team applied PLAID, an mT5 reranker, GPT-4 reranker, score fusion, and document translation to the TREC 2024 NeuCLIR track. For PLAID we included a variety of models and training techniques -- Translate Distill (TD), Generate Distill (GD) and multi-lingual translate-distill (MTD). TD uses scores from the mT5 model over English MS MARCO query-document pairs to learn how to score query-document pairs where the documents are translated to match the CLIR setting. GD follows TD but uses passages from the collection and queries generated by an LLM for training examples. MTD uses MS MARCO translated into multiple languages, allowing experiments on how to batch the data during training. Finally, for report generation we experimented with system combination over different runs. One family of systems used either GPT-4o or Claude-3.5-Sonnet to summarize the retrieved results from a series of decomposed sub-questions. Another system took the output from those two models and verified/combined them with Claude-3.5-Sonnet. The other family used GPT4o and GPT3.5Turbo to extract and group relevant facts from the retrieved documents based on the decomposed queries. The resulting submissions directly concatenate the grouped facts to form the report and their documents of origin as the citations. The team submitted runs to all NeuCLIR tasks: CLIR and MLIR news tasks as well as the technical documents task and the report generation task."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"TREC 2024 System Paper; 6 pages; 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00143","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:52:30Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:52:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:52:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00129","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00129","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00129","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengkui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hengkui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liujiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Liujiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihua","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qihao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keke","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Keke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuyang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Shuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renle","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Renle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dahao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Dahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingyu","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Lingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruce","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bruce","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqiang","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Jiaqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinglong","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Xinglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yaqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjun","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Wenjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Panpan","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Panpan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ken","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ken","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hengyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingsi","familyName":"Xin","name":"Xin, Yingsi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongfeng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yongfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuexin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manqi","familyName":"Ruan","name":"Ruan, Manqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ningtao","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Ningtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohua","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiaohua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weipeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Weipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BigBang-Proton Technical Report: Next-Word-Prediction is Scientific Multitask Learner"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.7; J.2; I.6.3; K.4.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T05, 68T50, 00A69, 94A99","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T18:09:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:03:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce BigBang-Proton, a unified sequence-based architecture for auto-regressive language modeling pretrained on cross-scale, cross-structure, cross-discipline real-world scientific tasks to construct a scientific multi-task learner. BigBang-Proton incorporates three fundamental innovations compared to mainstream general-purpose LLMs: Theory-Experiment Learning paradigm aligns large-scale numerical experimental data with theoretical text corpora; Binary Patch Encoding replaces byte pair encoding(BPE) tokenization; Monte Carlo Attention substitutes traditional transformer architectures. Through next-word-prediction pretraining on cross-discipline scientific datasets of real-world problems mixed with general textual corpus, followed by fine-tuning and inference on downstream tasks, BigBang-Proton demonstrates 100\\% accuracy in up to 50-digit arithmetic addition operations, performance on par with leading specialized models in particle physics jet tagging, matching MAE of specialized models in inter-atomic potential simulation, performance comparable to traditional spatiotemporal models in water quality prediction, and benchmark-exceeding performance in genome modeling. These results prove that language-guided scientific computing can match or exceed the performance of task-specific scientific models while maintaining multitask learning capabilities. We further hypothesize to scale the pretraining to the universe scale as a fundamental step toward developing material world foundational model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"93 pages, 39 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00129","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:52:09Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:52:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:52:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00125","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00125","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00125","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong kyu","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hong kyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruixuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ruixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Direct Token Optimization: A Self-contained Approach to Large Language Model Unlearning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T18:05:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:03:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Machine unlearning is an emerging technique that removes the influence of a subset of training data (forget set) from a model without full retraining, with applications including privacy protection, content moderation, and model correction. The key challenge lies in ensuring that the model completely forgets the knowledge of the forget set without compromising its overall utility. Existing unlearning methods for large language models (LLMs) often utilize auxiliary language models, retain datasets, or even commercial AI services for effective unlearning and maintaining the model utility. However, dependence on these external resources is often impractical and could potentially introduce additional privacy risks. In this work, we propose direct token optimization (DTO), a novel self-contained unlearning approach for LLMs that directly optimizes the token level objectives and eliminates the need for external resources. Given a sequence to unlearn, we identify two categories of tokens: target tokens, which capture critical knowledge for unlearning, and the remaining non-target tokens, which are crucial for maintaining the model utility. The former are used to optimize the unlearning objective, while the latter serve to preserve the model's performance. The experimental results show that the proposed DTO achieves up to 16.8$\\times$ improvement in forget quality on several benchmark datasets than the latest baselines while maintaining a comparable level of model utility."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00125","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:52:03Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:52:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:52:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00088","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00088","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00088","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sagnik","familyName":"Basu","name":"Basu, Sagnik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubham","familyName":"Prakash","name":"Prakash, Shubham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashish Maruti","familyName":"Barge","name":"Barge, Ashish Maruti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddharth D","familyName":"Jaiswal","name":"Jaiswal, Siddharth D","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhisek","familyName":"Dash","name":"Dash, Abhisek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saptarshi","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Saptarshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Animesh","familyName":"Mukherjee","name":"Mukherjee, Animesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Judging by Appearances? Auditing and Intervening Vision-Language Models for Bail Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T12:11:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:02:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have been extensively used for legal judgment prediction tasks based on case reports and crime history. However, with a surge in the availability of large vision language models (VLMs), legal judgment prediction systems can now be made to leverage the images of the criminals in addition to the textual case reports/crime history. Applications built in this way could lead to inadvertent consequences and be used with malicious intent. In this work, we run an audit to investigate the efficiency of standalone VLMs in the bail decision prediction task. We observe that the performance is poor across multiple intersectional groups and models \\textit{wrongly deny bail to deserving individuals with very high confidence}. We design different intervention algorithms by first including legal precedents through a RAG pipeline and then fine-tuning the VLMs using innovative schemes. We demonstrate that these interventions substantially improve the performance of bail prediction. Our work paves the way for the design of smarter interventions on VLMs in the future, before they can be deployed for real-world legal judgment prediction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00088","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:51:09Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:51:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:51:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00079","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00079","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00079","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hai","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Hai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Directed Information $γ$-covering: An Information-Theoretic Framework for Context Engineering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T02:41:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce \\textbf{Directed Information $γ$-covering}, a simple but general framework for redundancy-aware context engineering. Directed information (DI), a causal analogue of mutual information, measures asymmetric predictiveness between chunks. If $\\operatorname{DI}_{i \\to j} \\ge H(C_j) - γ$, then $C_i$ suffices to represent $C_j$ up to $γ$ bits. Building on this criterion, we formulate context selection as a $γ$-cover problem and propose a greedy algorithm with provable guarantees: it preserves query information within bounded slack, inherits $(1+\\ln n)$ and $(1-1/e)$ approximations from submodular set cover, and enforces a diversity margin. Importantly, building the $γ$-cover is \\emph{query-agnostic}: it incurs no online cost and can be computed once offline and amortized across all queries. Experiments on HotpotQA show that $γ$-covering consistently improves over BM25, a competitive baseline, and provides clear advantages in hard-decision regimes such as context compression and single-slot prompt selection. These results establish DI $γ$-covering as a principled, self-organizing backbone for modern LLM pipelines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 6 tables, preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00079","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:56Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:50:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00069","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00069","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00069","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiancong","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Jiancong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuomeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhuomeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixun","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Zixun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuedong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yuedong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OIG-Bench: A Multi-Agent Annotated Benchmark for Multimodal One-Image Guides Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T15:44:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have demonstrated impressive capabilities. However, evaluating their capacity for human-like understanding in One-Image Guides remains insufficiently explored. One-Image Guides are a visual format combining text, imagery, and symbols to present reorganized and structured information for easier comprehension, which are specifically designed for human viewing and inherently embody the characteristics of human perception and understanding. Here, we present OIG-Bench, a comprehensive benchmark focused on One-Image Guide understanding across diverse domains. To reduce the cost of manual annotation, we developed a semi-automated annotation pipeline in which multiple intelligent agents collaborate to generate preliminary image descriptions, assisting humans in constructing image-text pairs. With OIG-Bench, we have conducted a comprehensive evaluation of 29 state-of-the-art MLLMs, including both proprietary and open-source models. The results show that Qwen2.5-VL-72B performs the best among the evaluated models, with an overall accuracy of 77%. Nevertheless, all models exhibit notable weaknesses in semantic understanding and logical reasoning, indicating that current MLLMs still struggle to accurately interpret complex visual-text relationships. In addition, we also demonstrate that the proposed multi-agent annotation system outperforms all MLLMs in image captioning, highlighting its potential as both a high-quality image description generator and a valuable tool for future dataset construction. Datasets are available at https://github.com/XiejcSYSU/OIG-Bench."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00069","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:41Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:50:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00067","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00067","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00067","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafael da Silva","familyName":"Maciel","name":"Maciel, Rafael da Silva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucio","familyName":"Veraldo","name":"Veraldo, Lucio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Intelligent 5S Audit: Application of Artificial Intelligence for Continuous Improvement in the Automotive Industry"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.1; H.4.2; J.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T05, 90B30","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T15:28:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The evolution of the 5S methodology with the support of artificial intelligence techniques represents a significant opportunity to improve industrial organization audits in the automotive chain, making them more objective, efficient and aligned with Industry 4.0 standards. This work developed an automated 5S audit system based on large-scale language models (LLM), capable of assessing the five senses (Seiri, Seiton, Seiso, Seiketsu, Shitsuke) in a standardized way through intelligent image analysis. The system's reliability was validated using Cohen's concordance coefficient (kappa = 0.75), showing strong alignment between the automated assessments and the corresponding human audits. The results indicate that the proposed solution contributes significantly to continuous improvement in automotive manufacturing environments, speeding up the audit process by 50% of the traditional time and maintaining the consistency of the assessments, with a 99.8% reduction in operating costs compared to traditional manual audits. The methodology presented establishes a new paradigm for integrating lean systems with emerging AI technologies, offering scalability for implementation in automotive plants of different sizes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 5 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00067","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:38Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:50:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00061","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00061","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00061","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdul","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Abdul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bumshik","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Bumshik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Survey of AI-Powered Approaches for Osteoporosis Diagnosis in Medical Imaging"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T06:01:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Osteoporosis silently erodes skeletal integrity worldwide; however, early detection through imaging can prevent most fragility fractures. Artificial intelligence (AI) methods now mine routine Dual-energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DXA), X-ray, Computed Tomography (CT), and Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) scans for subtle, clinically actionable markers, but the literature is fragmented. This survey unifies the field through a tri-axial framework that couples imaging modalities with clinical tasks and AI methodologies (classical machine learning, convolutional neural networks (CNNs), transformers, self-supervised learning, and explainable AI). Following a concise clinical and technical primer, we detail our Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA)-guided search strategy, introduce the taxonomy via a roadmap figure, and synthesize cross-study insights on data scarcity, external validation, and interpretability. By identifying emerging trends, open challenges, and actionable research directions, this review provides AI scientists, medical imaging researchers, and musculoskeletal clinicians with a clear compass to accelerate rigorous, patient-centered innovation in osteoporosis care. The project page of this survey can also be found on Github."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"56 pages, 18 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00061","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:29Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:50:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00047","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00047","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00047","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sihao","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Sihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santosh","familyName":"Vasa","name":"Vasa, Santosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditi","familyName":"Ramadwar","name":"Ramadwar, Aditi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Explanation-Driven Counterfactual Testing for Faithfulness in Vision-Language Model Explanations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T15:16:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) often produce fluent Natural Language Explanations (NLEs) that sound convincing but may not reflect the causal factors driving predictions. This mismatch of plausibility and faithfulness poses technical and governance risks. We introduce Explanation-Driven Counterfactual Testing (EDCT), a fully automated verification procedure for a target VLM that treats the model's own explanation as a falsifiable hypothesis. Given an image-question pair, EDCT: (1) obtains the model's answer and NLE, (2) parses the NLE into testable visual concepts, (3) generates targeted counterfactual edits via generative inpainting, and (4) computes a Counterfactual Consistency Score (CCS) using LLM-assisted analysis of changes in both answers and explanations. Across 120 curated OK-VQA examples and multiple VLMs, EDCT uncovers substantial faithfulness gaps and provides regulator-aligned audit artifacts indicating when cited concepts fail causal tests."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 workshop on Regulatable ML"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00047","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:08Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:50:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00046","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00046","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00046","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaotian","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Xiaotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reinforcement Learning-Based Prompt Template Stealing for Text-to-Image Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T12:29:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have transformed text-to-image workflows, allowing designers to create novel visual concepts with unprecedented speed. This progress has given rise to a thriving prompt trading market, where curated prompts that induce trademark styles are bought and sold. Although commercially attractive, prompt trading also introduces a largely unexamined security risk: the prompts themselves can be stolen.\n In this paper, we expose this vulnerability and present RLStealer, a reinforcement learning based prompt inversion framework that recovers its template from only a small set of example images. RLStealer treats template stealing as a sequential decision making problem and employs multiple similarity based feedback signals as reward functions to effectively explore the prompt space. Comprehensive experiments on publicly available benchmarks demonstrate that RLStealer gets state-of-the-art performance while reducing the total attack cost to under 13% of that required by existing baselines. Our further analysis confirms that RLStealer can effectively generalize across different image styles to efficiently steal unseen prompt templates. Our study highlights an urgent security threat inherent in prompt trading and lays the groundwork for developing protective standards in the emerging MLLMs marketplace."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00046","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:06Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:50:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00032","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00032","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00032","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyi","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Ziyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Zhenyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixi","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yixi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xidong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junying","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Junying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongsheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Rongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yipeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siqi","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Siqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benyou","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Benyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiguo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhiguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haizhou","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WaveMind: Towards a Conversational EEG Foundation Model Aligned to Textual and Visual Modalities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T06:21:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Electroencephalography (EEG) interpretation using multimodal large language models (MLLMs) offers a novel approach for analyzing brain signals. However, the complex nature of brain activity introduces critical challenges: EEG signals simultaneously encode both cognitive processes and intrinsic neural states, creating a mismatch in EEG paired-data modality that hinders effective cross-modal representation learning. Through a pivot investigation, we uncover complementary relationships between these modalities. Leveraging this insight, we propose mapping EEG signals and their corresponding modalities into a unified semantic space to achieve generalized interpretation. To fully enable conversational capabilities, we further introduce WaveMind-Instruct-338k, the first cross-task EEG dataset for instruction tuning. The resulting model demonstrates robust classification accuracy while supporting flexible, open-ended conversations across four downstream tasks, thereby offering valuable insights for both neuroscience research and the development of general-purpose EEG models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00032","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:46Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:49:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00028","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00028","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00028","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haocheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaofan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sitao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Sitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking RoPE Scaling in Quantized LLM: Theory, Outlier, and Channel-Band Analysis with Weight Rescaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T01:23:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Extending the context window support of large language models (LLMs) is crucial for tasks with long-distance dependencies. RoPE-based interpolation and extrapolation methods, such as linear scaling and frequency-aware schemes, enable longer input length support without retraining, while post-training quantization (PTQ) makes deployment practical. However, we show that combining RoPE position interpolation (PI) with PTQ degrades accuracy due to coupled effects including long-context aliasing, dynamic-range dilation, anisotropy from axis-aligned quantizers vs. rotated RoPE pairs, and outlier shifting that produces position-dependent logit noise. We provide, to the best of our knowledge, the first systematic analysis of the PI+PTQ approach and introduce two practical diagnostics: interpolation pressure (per-band sensitivity to phase scaling) and tail-inflation ratios (outlier shift from short to long contexts). Following the analysis results, we propose Q-ROAR (Quantization, RoPE-interpolation, and Outlier Aware Rescaling), a weight-only, interpolation-aware stabilization of PI for quantized LLMs. Q-ROAR groups RoPE dimensions into a small number of frequency bands and performs a lightweight search over per-band scales for Key and Query weights (with an optional symmetric variant to preserve logit scale). The search is guided by our diagnostics and uses a tiny long-context development dataset, requiring no fine-tuning to the model, no architecture or kernel changes, and no additional deployment overhead. Empirically, Q-ROAR reduces the model's perplexity on long-context workloads by more than 14%, while preserving short-context performance, inference throughput, and compatibility with existing LLM system stacks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00028","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:40Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:49:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00013","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00013","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00013","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikai","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyang","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Haoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lining","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Lining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanbin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yanbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Guang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ProTDyn: a foundation Protein language model for Thermodynamics and Dynamics generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Biological Physics (physics.bio-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-17T03:24:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Molecular dynamics (MD) simulation has long been the principal computational tool for exploring protein conformational landscapes and dynamics, but its application is limited by high computational cost. We present ProTDyn, a foundation protein language model that unifies conformational ensemble generation and multi-timescale dynamics modeling within a single framework. Unlike prior approaches that treat these tasks separately, ProTDyn allows flexible independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) ensemble sampling and dynamic trajectory simulation. Across diverse protein systems, ProTDyn yields thermodynamically consistent ensembles, faithfully reproduces dynamical properties over multiple timescales, and generalizes to proteins beyond its training data. It offers a scalable and efficient alternative to conventional MD simulations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00013","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:18Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:49:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00001","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00001","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00001","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah","familyName":"Broestl","name":"Broestl, Noah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adel Nasser","familyName":"Abdalla","name":"Abdalla, Adel Nasser","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajprakash","familyName":"Bale","name":"Bale, Rajprakash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hersh","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Hersh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Struever","name":"Struever, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Methodological Framework for Quantifying Semantic Test Coverage in RAG Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-13T19:48:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reliably determining the performance of Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems depends on comprehensive test questions. While a proliferation of evaluation frameworks for LLM-powered applications exists, current practices lack a systematic method to ensure these test sets adequately cover the underlying knowledge base, leaving developers with significant blind spots. To address this, we present a novel, applied methodology to quantify the semantic coverage of RAG test questions against their underlying documents. Our approach leverages existing technologies, including vector embeddings and clustering algorithms, to create a practical framework for validating test comprehensiveness. Our methodology embeds document chunks and test questions into a unified vector space, enabling the calculation of multiple coverage metrics: basic proximity, content-weighted coverage, and multi-topic question coverage. Furthermore, we incorporate outlier detection to filter irrelevant questions, allowing for the refinement of test sets. Experimental evidence from two distinct use cases demonstrates that our framework effectively quantifies test coverage, identifies specific content areas with inadequate representation, and provides concrete recommendations for generating new, high-value test questions. This work provides RAG developers with essential tools to build more robust test suites, thereby improving system reliability and extending to applications such as identifying misaligned documents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 3 figures, 1 table, 1 algo"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00001","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:00Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:49:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":246,"totalPages":3,"page":3},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-01T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-02T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=3\u0026sort=registered"}}